// Alternate History: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== alternate-history.f01 | feature | three-act
title: Thirteen
genre: alternate-history + drama
tone: Chrome-bright, level-headed and quietly thrilling
era: 1962 | period
setting: A desert clinic, a naval air station and a launch complex, in a 1962 that went a little differently | fictional | institution
logline: In a world where one telegram was never sent, thirteen women pilots who privately passed the same medical tests as the first astronauts are allowed to finish the programme, and one of them is strapped into a capsule for an orbit nobody in charge wanted her to fly.
premise: The point of divergence is small and stated in the first minute: the navy does not cancel the women's jet-orientation week at its air station, because the officer who would have asked headquarters whether the tests were an official requirement is in hospital with appendicitis. So the tests go on. The results are better than anyone is comfortable with: the women are lighter, use less oxygen, and outlast the men in the isolation tank by hours.
synopsis: The first act is the tests as they were: the tilt table, ice water in the ear, a rubber tube swallowed, the dark silent tank. {gerry}, a flight instructor with ten thousand hours and three part-time jobs, floats in it for ten hours and has to be asked to come out. The second act is the week at the air station that never happened: altitude chambers, ejection-seat rigs, the first jet rides. The film has fun with capability and makes no speeches. The women are not saints; {jan_d}, wealthy, the best known of them, wants to be first and lobbies senators, while {gerry} wants only for someone to go. The agency's position is that astronauts must be military jet test pilots, a profession closed to women; its most famous man, {the_colonel}, a decent hero, says in a hearing that it is simply a fact of the social order. In our world that ended it. Here, the appendicitis has consequences: there is data now, a navy captain willing to sign it, and a rival power about to launch a woman for the headline. The administrator, {mr_w}, a politician before he is anything else, does the sum. One flight, three orbits, a capsule already built, presented as a medical study. The astronaut office is furious and then, being professionals, helps. The third act is the flight and its ordinary difficulty. The candidate is {gerry}, chosen because she is the best and because she will not make trouble. {jan_d} is capsule communicator and has to be gracious on an open line. There is a stuck valve, a warning light, a manual retrofire: exactly what the men faced, handled the same way. The film's alternate history is deliberately modest. She lands. There is a parade. The rules are not rewritten for another ten years, but they are rewritten ten years sooner. The last scene returns to the clinic's dark tank, where a girl of twelve on a school visit asks how long the record is, and is told.
question: How much of history is a qualification nobody was allowed to earn?
themes: capability against category; the small accident that opens a door; ambition among allies
audience: General; adults and teenagers
viewpoint: With {gerry}; the alternate world is signalled lightly, by what is on newsstands
stakes: One life on top of a rocket, thirteen careers, and what a generation of girls is told is possible
conflict: Qualified women against a definition of "qualified" written to exclude them, and against each other for one seat
ending: She flies three orbits and comes home. Little changes at once. Everything changes a decade early.
experience: The satisfaction of competence, some righteous irritation, a launch that grips, and a sly hopeful ending
look: Clinic white and desert glare; orange pressure suits; period television graphics; a silver capsule
sound: A heartbeat in a silent tank; a countdown; an open radio line with two women being careful
m: prove | institution | ambition-vs-integrity | triumph
@ gerry | Gerry Colvin | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 31 | For one of them to fly; herself, if it must be someone | Ten thousand hours and no one will let her near a jet | The rules were written before she was asked | Plain-spoken, devout, relentlessly capable | From test subject to pilot in command | Oklahoma vowels, checklists | Ponytail, a flight jacket, a silver capsule's reflection
@ jan_d | Jan Darrow | co-lead | rival | human | she/her | 40 | To be first | She has eight children, a senator husband and a point to make | She is not chosen | Polished, formidable, generous in the end | From campaigner for herself to the voice on the line | Finishing-school crispness | Pearls with a flight suit
@ the_colonel | The Colonel | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 41 | The programme protected | He earned his seat the hard way | The data | Honourable, conventional, fair when cornered | From "a fact of our social order" to a hand on a capsule hatch | Clipped, modest, final | Crew cut, a bow tie at a hearing
@ mr_w | The Administrator | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 56 | Budgets and headlines | A rival power is about to launch a woman | His own astronauts | Genial, calculating | From refusal to a decision presented as inevitable | Southern courtesy | A three-piece suit, a slide rule he cannot use
@ doctor_l | Doctor Lowell | supporting | mentor | human | he/him | 55 | Good data | He designed the tests and wondered | He paid for this out of his own clinic | Curious, stubborn, apolitical | From private experiment to evidence at a hearing | Clinical, dry | White coat, a stopwatch
~ gerry - jan_d | two of thirteen | allies with one seat between them | one flies, one talks her down
~ gerry - the_colonel | applicant and hero | a category she is outside | he closes her hatch himself
~ gerry - doctor_l | subject and physician | a volunteer | ten hours in the tank, and a record
# The Tests | INT | Clinic, Isolation Tank | NIGHT | gerry, doctor_l | Float | Dark water at body heat. No sound. The men lasted three hours. At ten he knocks on the lid and asks if she would mind coming out | The results are too good | 6 | water, props=isolation tank
# The Tests | INT | Clinic, Tilt Table | DAY | gerry, jan_d, doctor_l | Pass | Ice water in the ear, a swallowed tube, a tilting table. Thirteen pass. {jan_d} is already drafting a letter to a senator | | 5 | crowd=13, props=medical apparatus
# The Week | EXT | Naval Air Station, Flight Line | DAY | gerry, jan_d | Not be cancelled | The telegram that was not sent. Jets on a ramp. An officer in hospital. A captain shrugs and signs the schedule | History diverges | 5 | vehicles=jet trainers, crowd=40
# The Week | INT | Altitude Chamber | DAY | gerry, jan_d | Outlast them | Pressure suits, a climbing needle, puzzles done at simulated height. The technician checks the gauge twice | | 5 | props=pressure suits
# The Hearing | INT | Congressional Hearing Room | DAY | gerry, jan_d, the_colonel, doctor_l, mr_w | Be told the order of things | The hero says the men go off and fly the planes; it is a fact of the social order. {doctor_l} puts a folder on the table. This time there is a navy signature in it | The data is on the record | 7 | crowd=150, props=folder
# The Hearing | INT | Administrator's Office | NIGHT | mr_w, the_colonel | Do the sum | A rival's launch date. A spare capsule. Three orbits, called a medical study. The colonel says it is a stunt. The administrator says: ours | She is given a flight | 5 | props=newspaper
# The Flight | INT | Capsule, On The Pad | DAWN | gerry, the_colonel, jan_d | Go | The colonel closes the hatch himself. {jan_d} on the line, gracious through her teeth, then simply good. A hold. A count | Launch | 7 | vehicles=rocket and capsule, vfx=launch
# The Flight | INT | Capsule, In Orbit | DAY | gerry, jan_d | Fly it by hand | A stuck valve, a light, the automatic system out. She takes the stick. Two women's voices doing a checklist over an ocean | She brings it home manually | 7 | vfx=earth from orbit, props=capsule controls
# The Flight | INT | Clinic, Isolation Tank | DAY | doctor_l | Tell the record | Years on. A school visit. A girl of twelve asks how long anyone stayed in there. He tells her who, and how long | | 3 | crowd=20
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; an invented history departing from the privately funded testing of women pilots in 1960 to 1961, with invented people
